WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.860
A cell copies its DNA before mitosis, so each new cell can get a full set of

2
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.120
instructions. Copy first, then share the copies.

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.660
Picture two groups that each need a whole recipe book.

4
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.500
Tearing a book in half gives each group only half the recipes.

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.420
Copying it first gives each a full book.

6
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.540
The comparison breaks because a cell does not decide anything.

7
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.100
Proteins copy and share the DNA.

8
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.060
In interphase, G1 is growth, S phase is DNA copying, and G2 is preparation for

9
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.740
division. Each copied chromosome has two identical sister chromatids, joined at the

10
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.740
centromere.

11
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.970
Mitosis shares the copied chromosomes between two nuclei.

12
00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:52.470
Spindle fibers are thin protein fibers that attach to chromosomes and move them.

13
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.270
In prophase, chromosomes coil up.

14
00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.570
Near the end, the nuclear envelope breaks down.

15
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:03.270
In metaphase, spindle fibers line the chromosomes up across the middle.

16
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.970
In anaphase, sister chromatids separate and move to opposite ends.

17
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.210
In telophase, a full set reaches each end.

18
00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.250
A new nuclear envelope forms around each set.

19
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.640
Cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm, making two cells.

20
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.860
It often starts as mitosis is ending.

21
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.720
In an animal cell, a ring pinches the membrane inward.

22
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.400
The furrow deepens until the cell splits.

23
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.600
A plant cell has a stiff wall and cannot pinch.

24
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.180
A cell plate forms across the middle and grows outward into a new wall.

25
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.120
The two new cells usually have the same DNA.

26
00:01:40.283 --> 00:01:44.463
These new cells let living things grow, repair damage and replace cells.

27
00:01:45.523 --> 00:01:49.003
Skin cells divide behind a wound to replace cells that were lost.

28
00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:52.003
Near an onion root tip, cells divide.

29
00:01:52.923 --> 00:01:56.523
Behind that zone, the new cells stretch, pushing the root through the soil.

30
00:01:57.403 --> 00:02:01.523
For a single-celled amoeba or yeast cell, division can make a new organism.

31
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:04.937
A smaller point is why cells stay small.

32
00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:07.977
Oxygen, food and wastes cross the membrane.

33
00:02:08.877 --> 00:02:13.517
In a cube model, as a cell gets bigger, its volume grows faster than its surface.

34
00:02:14.377 --> 00:02:16.337
It has less membrane for each bit inside.

35
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:18.457
Real cells are not cubes.

36
00:02:19.517 --> 00:02:23.637
Cells also control their size, so surface area is one limit among several.
